title:          "Conflict outcomes and the effectiveness of bottom-up democracy aid. An analysis of post-conflict countries, 1947-2013" 
date:           2026-01-01
category:       "Work in Progress"
abstract: >-
          Democracy aid is often disbursed to countries recovering from civil war to promote transitions to democracy and bottom-up democracy aid – assistance targeted at strengthening and democratizing civil society – is prioritized in post-conflict settings. Despite negative expectations about democracy aid effectiveness, there is variation in the aid’s success in post-conflict countries. What explains these differences in the degree to which aid promotes democracy remains unclear. I argue that the way in which the preceding civil war was terminated affects the recipient elites’ incentives to implement bottom-up democracy aid. Democratization reforms create three main costs for non-democratic incumbents: a loss of political power, uncertainty about their future political power, and decreased access to state rents. Aid only incentivizes democratic reforms when the risks of not democratizing outweigh these costs for the incumbent. I expect the impact of bottom-up democracy aid on democratization to vary with three conflict outcomes - peace agreements, ceasefires and decisive victories – as these determine the balance of power between the incumbent and civil society in a post-conflict setting. The more equal this balance, as is the case after negotiated peace agreements, the more credible the threat of revolution from civil society that pushes for democratic reforms. This in turn raises the risks of not democratizing for the incumbent and incentives the implementation of bottom-up democracy aid to appease civil society. I test the argument by analyzing the effect of bottom-up democracy aid on democracy levels for all post-conflict countries between 1947 and 2013. The results reveal that conflict outcomes play an important role in shaping the impact of bottom-up democracy aid to post-conflict countries. Such aid is more effective in settings where civil wars are terminated through peace agreements and less effective after decisive victories by one side. The findings contribute to our understanding of why the effectiveness of democracy aid varies across post-conflict contexts.

authors:
  - Leonie Reicheneder
